{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The deliverables explicitly identify the GSA per-diem files webpage (https://www.gsa.gov/travel/plan-a-trip/per-diem-rates/per-diem-files) and reference the real rate/M&IE files linked from it: FY2025_PerDiemMasterRatesFile.xlsx, FY2026_PerDiemMasterRatesFile.xlsx, and GSA_FY2025_MIE_Breakdown.docx. These URLs match entries actually present in the input gsa_per_diem_files.html. The conference_budget.xlsx 'Sources' sheet provides a 'Content / Use in Budget' column explaining each file's role (lodging/M&IE caps, meal deductions, first/last-day 75% rate, etc.), and approval_brief.docx \u00a72 gives a parallel narrative mapping specific row numbers and values to the budget. Criterion satisfied."}

--- GO002 ---
{"score": "no", "reasoning": "GO002 requires three cities' daily rates to be correctly matched by county, season, and FY2025/FY2026, with row numbers from the original tables provided for both years. The FY2025 row citations are correct (Phoenix row 22 Sep 1-30 $160/$86; Denver row 109 Apr 1-Sep 30 $215/$92; Chicago row 258 Sep 1-30 $223/$92), and the dollar amounts themselves match. However, for FY2026, the candidate explicitly asserts in approval_brief.docx \u00a72 and conference_budget.xlsx Sources sheet that the \"same row numbers\" cover Oct 1 in the FY2026 file. This is factually wrong: in FY2026_PerDiemMasterRatesFile.xlsx, the Oct 1 season rows are Phoenix row 18 (Oct 1-Jan 31), Denver row 107 (Oct 1-Oct 31), and Chicago row 254 (Oct 1-Nov 30) \u2014 not rows 22/109/258 (which in the FY2026 file still represent the Sep 1-30 / Apr 1-Sep 30 / Sep 1-30 seasons and do not cover Oct 1). The FY2026 row citations in both deliverables are incorrect, so the \"correctly match + provide two years' row numbers\" requirement is not fully satisfied."}

--- GO003 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "All four components of GO003 are satisfied across the three deliverables. (1) Lodging is billed at actual quote, not cap: Daily sheet columns use Quoted Rate (218/209/155) rather than Lodging Cap (223/215/160); Options Per-Person Lodging+Tax per city = 4 nights \u00d7 (quote + 12% tax): Chicago 976.64, Denver 936.32, Phoenix 694.40 \u2014 all derived from quotes, not caps. (2) Four nights correct: Daily sheet has lodging rows for 09-28, 09-29, 09-30, 10-01 (4 nights) and marks 10-02 'N/A (no lodging)'; brief Section 1 states 'four nights lodging'. (3) 12% tax calculated separately: Tax column distinct from quoted rate (26.16 = 12%\u00d7218; 25.08 = 12%\u00d7209; 18.60 = 12%\u00d7155); brief \u00a73 states 'Lodging tax = 12% of quoted rate'. (4) Tax does not exceed reimbursable base / cap is respected: brief explicitly states 'cap applies to the pre-tax lodging rate; quoted rates are below all caps \u2192 no actual-expense authorization needed', and approval_register.csv row '\u5b9e\u9645\u8d39\u7528\u62a5\u9500\u6388\u6743' confirms '\u672c\u65b9\u6848\u5747\u65e0\u9700' (none needed). Sources sheet cites FTR 301-11.100 reinforcing that cap applies to pre-tax rate and tax is separate. All three deliverables are internally consistent."}

--- GO004 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "All three deliverables satisfy GO004. In conference_budget.xlsx (Daily sheet): First/last days correctly use 75% of full M&IE as the base (Chicago/Denver: 0.75*92=69; Phoenix: 0.75*86=64.5), then deduct the FULL government meal amount ($38 dinner, $26/$23 lunch) \u2014 not 75% of the meal. Middle days correctly use the full M&IE rate and deduct applicable registration-included and/or government-furnished meals. All net M&IE values remain above the $5 incidental-expenses floor (minimum is $27 Phoenix 10-01, well above floor). approval_brief.docx explicitly states the 75% rule and floor in Section 3 ('On first/last day the deduction applies against the reduced (75%) M&IE rate; floor = incidental expenses ($5)'), with correct per-city 5-day M&IE totals (Chicago/Denver $234, Phoenix $223) matching the Excel. approval_register.csv includes an M&IE reimbursement guidance row requiring daily meal-deduction disclosure. All three files are internally consistent."}

--- GO005 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "Criterion GO005 requires: (1) hotel free breakfast and tea breaks are NOT deducted, and (2) government meal dates and meal types are ALL used. \n\nPart 1 - Satisfied: The candidate's budget shows zero breakfast deductions across all dates and cities. No refreshment/tea/coffee deductions appear. The approval brief explicitly states in section 3: 'Hotel free breakfast: no M&IE deduction (301-11.17). Coffee/fruit breaks: no deduction (301-74.7).' This matches the golden output which shows hotel_breakfast_deduction=0 and refreshment_deduction=0 for all rows.\n\nPart 2 - Satisfied: All government-furnished meals are properly deducted:\n- 09-28: Dinner (Gov welcome dinner) for all 3 cities ($38/$36)\n- 10-01: Dinner (Gov) for all 3 cities ($38/$36)  \n- 10-02: Lunch (Gov departure lunch) for all 3 cities ($26/$23)\n\nPlus registration-included lunches on 09-29, 09-30, 10-01 are also correctly deducted. The deduction amounts match the golden output exactly. All government meal dates (09-28, 10-01, 10-02) and meal types (dinner, lunch) are accounted for."}

--- GO006 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "Criterion GO006 focuses on the six options' headcount, airfare, local transport, registration, and fixed venue fees having no duplication/omission, and the base subtotal being accurate to the cent. Verified across conference_budget.xlsx (Options sheet) and approval_brief.docx (Section 4): all 6 base options (3 cities \u00d7 2 headcounts) are present with consistent per-person line items within each city (Phoenix: airfare 510, transport 90, reg 180, venue 8000; Denver: 340/70/180/10500; Chicago: 410/80/180/9500). Computed base subtotals match stated values to the cent: Phoenix N=18 $38,553.20, Denver N=18 $42,185.76, Chicago N=18 $43,351.52, Phoenix N=24 $48,737.60, Denver N=24 $52,747.68, Chicago N=24 $54,635.36. Each equals N \u00d7 (lodging+tax + airfare + transport + reg + M&IE) + venue. No duplication between N=18 and N=24 rows, no omission of any of the 6 options. Note: approval_register.csv has an anomalous FY26 N=24 figure ($10,174.48) not matching any option's FY26 w/5% cost, but GO006's scope is the 6 options' listed line items and base subtotal, all of which are correct."}

--- GO007 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "All deliverables correctly implement GO007. The Options sheet correctly computes 5% reserve on base subtotal, compares total (base + reserve) against $53,000 cap, and marks feasibility. The six base options are correctly filtered: all pass lodging compliance; Chicago 24 and Denver 24 fail total cap ($57,367.13 and $55,385.06 > $53,000). Among 4 feasible options, headcount is prioritized (Phoenix 24 max at N=24), then lowest total with reserve within same headcount (Phoenix 18 at $40,480.86 is lowest among N=18). The approval_brief.docx section 8 correctly states unconditional approval for Phoenix N=18 and conditional approval for Phoenix N=24 requiring written FY26\u2192FY2025 transfer authorization. The approval_register.csv correctly lists the transfer as a required condition for N=24."}

--- GO008 ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "Criterion GO008 is fully satisfied. (1) Six schemes with airfare +20%: All 6 combinations (3 cities \u00d7 2 headcounts) present in stress scenario table with correct calculations matching golden reference within rounding. (2) Calculations verified: Chicago N=18 base $44,827.52, N=24 base $56,603.36; Denver N=18 base $43,409.76, N=24 base $54,379.68; Phoenix N=18 base $40,389.20, N=24 base $51,185.60. All totals correctly apply 5% reserve on stressed base (not frozen). (3) Executable exceedance measures provided: (a) lock airfare via early booking/city-pair contract, (b) cap attendance at 18 if airfare rises, (c) seek FY2025 augmentation approval if 24 attendees essential, (d) re-run comparison with actual airfare. All measures are practical, actionable, and address the stress scenario appropriately. (4) GO-D2 claim correctly identified as FALSE with proper corrective action (recompute reserve on revised base)."}

